---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MediaCredit from "../components/MediaCredit.astro";
import site from "../data/site.json";
import { asset } from "../data/paths";
---

<Layout title={`Accessibility & media credits — ${site.wordmark}`}>
  <div class="reading-container">
    <header class="page-title">
      <p class="eyebrow">Access & attribution</p><h1>
        Accessibility & media credits
      </h1><p class="lead">The design target is WCAG 2.1 Level A and AA.</p><p>
        This author-maintained research site uses semantic HTML, a neutral
        palette, keyboard-operable controls, and descriptive alternatives for
        research media. Automated checks are part of the deployment workflow;
        they do not establish complete ADA or WCAG conformance.
      </p>
    </header>
    <div class="prose">
      <section>
        <h2>Using this site</h2><ul>
          <li>
            The first keyboard link skips to the main content. Keyboard focus is
            visible throughout the page.
          </li><li>
            Videos start only when you choose to play them. Native player
            controls remain available. With JavaScript enabled, separate
            play/pause/replay buttons control the title pair and the experiment
            highlights. No video loops automatically.
          </li><li>
            All three videos are silent and retain the original playback speed:
            the approximately 36-second field recording, a 12-second G1 excerpt,
            and 33 seconds of G1 highlights from three separate recordings.
            Complete visual descriptions and timed WebVTT description tracks are
            provided for each video.
          </li><li>
            On larger screens, two videos appear behind the title. Choose “Show
            full video frames” to move the title above the complete, unobscured
            frames. Narrow screens place the title above the videos
            automatically; on phones, the videos stack vertically. Native
            fullscreen controls and downloads also provide the complete frames.
          </li><li>
            The experiment highlights use labeled clips and visible cuts rather
            than one continuous trial. The safety tether, operator, and
            gantry-assisted resets remain visible. The video description
            explains the sequence without requiring playback.
          </li><li>
            Paper figures have descriptive alternatives and longer explanations.
            Results appear as labeled values and structured tables, so color is
            not required to understand them.
          </li><li>
            At narrow widths, content reflows into a single column. Wide data
            tables and BibTeX text scroll within their own focusable areas.
          </li><li>
            Core content, result comparisons, native video controls, media
            downloads, and full text alternatives remain available without
            JavaScript. The paired playback and title-view buttons require
            JavaScript.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>
      <section id="attachments">
        <h2>Papers, figures & data attachments</h2><p>
          The <a href={asset("paper/")}>HTML research guide</a> is a companion to
          the paper, not its complete text. <a href={asset("results/")}
            >HTML result tables and figure descriptions</a
          > accompany the downloadable CSV data and images.
        </p><p>
          The supplied roofing manuscript PDF is untagged. We link to the exact
          arXiv v1 record, its original PDF, and arXiv’s experimental full-text
          HTML rendering. The HTML research guide on this website covers
          selected content only; it does not replace the full paper.
        </p><p>
          The original uploaded PDF has not been altered or republished here. We
          have not certified external PDF reading order, mathematical
          accessibility, or assistive-technology behavior. CSV files are plain
          data; the HTML tables provide captions and row/column headers.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2>Checks and remaining review</h2><p>
          The repository includes an axe-core check against WCAG 2.1 A/AA rules,
          plus checks for keyboard navigation, media behavior, image
          alternatives, 320 CSS-pixel reflow, text spacing, and content with
          JavaScript disabled. A failing check blocks automatic deployment. See <a
            href={`${site.siteRepo}/actions`}>the deployment checks</a
          > and <a href={`${site.siteRepo}/blob/main/ACCESSIBILITY.md`}
            >the review record</a
          >.
        </p><p>
          The 11 October 2026 revision adds the paired title videos and edited
          physical-experiment highlights. Local production-preview testing passed the axe scans and functional
          checks with no recorded violations or errors, including pause controls
          while videos are buffering. The linked
          review record distinguishes these technical checks from the remaining
          manual review and records the deployed revision.
        </p><p>
          Automated coverage is limited. A full review with multiple screen
          readers, users with disabilities, and institutional accessibility and
          brand reviewers remains outside the completed technical checks. This
          page does not claim an ADA or WCAG certification.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2>Report an access barrier</h2><p>
          Email <a href="mailto:liusongyang@ufl.edu"
            >Songyang Liu at liusongyang@ufl.edu</a
          >. Please include the page, the content you need, and the browser or
          assistive technology involved so we can help provide a usable format.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2>Institutional identity & privacy</h2><p>
          Author affiliations are reproduced from the paper. The website does
          not use the UF logo, seal, or blue/orange identity palette. The
          institutional disclaimer appears on every page. A neutral visual
          design is not a substitute for institutional brand approval.
        </p><p>
          This static site loads its fonts, figures, and all three research-team
          videos locally. It uses no analytics, advertising, or embedded
          third-party players. Following an external link opens the provider’s
          site, where that provider’s privacy practices apply.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
    <div class="section"><MediaCredit /></div>
    <section class="section">
      <h2>Research figure credits</h2><p>
        Selected figures come from the user-supplied arXiv preprint by {
          site.authors.map((a) => a.name).join(", ")
        }. Figure numbers, original third-party credits where present, and
        descriptive explanations appear on the <a
          href={asset("results/#figures")}>results and figures page</a
        >. Rights in research papers and third-party media remain with their
        respective holders.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>
